WEBVTT

00:00:00.261 --> 00:00:01.890
Last video ended with this fence.

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:03.956
We'll solve it later, once we can build

00:00:03.976 --> 00:00:05.641
a function from a situation like this.

00:00:06.504 --> 00:00:08.432
First, there's a gap in our derivative list.

00:00:08.996 --> 00:00:11.350
We know sine and cos, but not tan

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:11.531
x.

00:00:12.317 --> 00:00:12.983
Here's its graph.

00:00:13.588 --> 00:00:16.457
It runs between vertical asymptotes, walls the curve

00:00:16.478 --> 00:00:16.919
never meets,

00:00:17.442 --> 00:00:19.428
at minus pi over two and pi over

00:00:19.449 --> 00:00:19.648
two.

00:00:20.476 --> 00:00:21.364
Watch its tangent.

00:00:22.193 --> 00:00:23.700
Coming in from the left it's steep.

00:00:24.364 --> 00:00:26.468
It eases to its flattest at zero, where

00:00:26.509 --> 00:00:28.614
the slope is one, then steepens again.

00:00:29.398 --> 00:00:31.150
It never gets flatter than that, and it

00:00:31.272 --> 00:00:31.896
never falls.

00:00:32.702 --> 00:00:34.816
So why is the slope never below one?

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:36.854
And what is it, exactly?

00:00:37.657 --> 00:00:39.732
Let's measure some slopes on this middle piece.

00:00:40.418 --> 00:00:42.190
At zero, the height is zero and the

00:00:42.231 --> 00:00:43.016
slope is one.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.744
At pi over four, the height is one

00:00:45.885 --> 00:00:46.847
and the slope is two.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:49.734
At pi over three, the height is root

00:00:49.776 --> 00:00:52.201
three, about one point seven three, and the

00:00:52.242 --> 00:00:53.183
slope is four.

00:00:53.851 --> 00:00:54.419
Look at the pattern.

00:00:55.007 --> 00:00:56.355
Height zero, slope one.

00:00:56.861 --> 00:00:58.152
Height one, slope two.

00:00:58.759 --> 00:01:00.808
Height root three, slope four.

00:01:01.513 --> 00:01:03.585
Each slope is one more than the height

00:01:03.646 --> 00:01:04.068
squared.

00:01:04.794 --> 00:01:06.103
Hold on to that, because the algebra is

00:01:06.145 --> 00:01:06.890
about to explain it.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:07.791
Why?

00:01:08.798 --> 00:01:11.024
Tan x is sine x over cos x,

00:01:11.607 --> 00:01:12.027
a quotient.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.356
The quotient rule video split a quotient's slope

00:01:15.437 --> 00:01:16.459
into two turns.

00:01:17.042 --> 00:01:19.096
The top's turn is the top's slope over

00:01:19.138 --> 00:01:19.520
the bottom.

00:01:20.003 --> 00:01:22.128
That's cos x over cos x, which is

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:23.792
exactly one, everywhere.

00:01:24.476 --> 00:01:25.822
That's where the one comes from.

00:01:26.893 --> 00:01:27.982
Now the bottom's turn.

00:01:28.646 --> 00:01:31.129
Between zero and pi over two, cos x

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:31.850
is shrinking,

00:01:32.352 --> 00:01:34.574
and a shrinking bottom makes a positive fraction

00:01:34.696 --> 00:01:35.075
grow,

00:01:35.577 --> 00:01:36.517
so this turn adds.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.223
It works out to sine squared over cos

00:01:39.244 --> 00:01:41.207
squared, which is tan squared x,

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:42.549
the height squared.

00:01:43.473 --> 00:01:45.338
At pi over four, that's one plus one,

00:01:45.921 --> 00:01:46.081
two.

00:01:46.745 --> 00:01:48.595
At pi over three, one plus three,

00:01:49.099 --> 00:01:49.340
four.

00:01:50.044 --> 00:01:52.271
And near pi over two, cos x gets

00:01:52.352 --> 00:01:53.214
close to zero,

00:01:53.797 --> 00:01:56.084
so the bottom's turn grows without any limit.

00:01:56.729 --> 00:01:57.761
That's the steep wall.

00:01:58.428 --> 00:02:00.636
The syllabus asks you to show this result,

00:02:01.038 --> 00:02:03.086
so here's the proof, line by line.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.354
Tan x is sine x over cos x,

00:02:06.776 --> 00:02:08.259
and cos x can't be zero.

00:02:08.942 --> 00:02:11.207
The quotient rule gives cos x times cos

00:02:11.268 --> 00:02:11.368
x,

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.457
minus sine x times minus sine x,

00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:16.603
all over cos squared x.

00:02:17.245 --> 00:02:19.329
The two minus signs make a plus, so

00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.015
the top is cos squared x plus sine

00:02:22.036 --> 00:02:22.557
squared x.

00:02:23.221 --> 00:02:25.855
By the Pythagorean identity, that top is one.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.907
So the derivative is one over cos squared

00:02:29.069 --> 00:02:29.128
x.

00:02:30.155 --> 00:02:31.845
One over cos x has its own name,

00:02:31.966 --> 00:02:32.509
sec x.

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:35.078
So the derivative of tan x is sec

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:35.860
squared x.

00:02:36.865 --> 00:02:38.511
And cos squared x is never more than

00:02:38.673 --> 00:02:41.103
one, so sec squared x is never less

00:02:41.144 --> 00:02:41.484
than one.

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:44.004
That's why the slope never drops below one.

00:02:44.495 --> 00:02:45.881
Two conditions come with this result.

00:02:46.445 --> 00:02:48.073
It's built from the derivatives of sine and

00:02:48.114 --> 00:02:50.224
cos, so x must be in radians.

00:02:50.968 --> 00:02:53.474
And tan x only exists where cos x

00:02:53.616 --> 00:02:56.162
isn't zero, so x can't be pi over

00:02:56.223 --> 00:02:58.168
two, plus any whole number of pis.

00:02:59.212 --> 00:03:02.606
The graph breaks into branches, unbroken pieces between

00:03:02.627 --> 00:03:03.530
neighbouring walls.

00:03:04.274 --> 00:03:07.702
Sec squared is always positive, so tan increases

00:03:07.804 --> 00:03:08.625
along each branch.

00:03:09.087 --> 00:03:10.363
But not across a wall.

00:03:11.168 --> 00:03:13.251
Tan of pi over four is one, but

00:03:13.292 --> 00:03:15.314
tan of three pi over four is minus

00:03:15.415 --> 00:03:17.638
one, even though three pi over four comes

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:17.999
later.

00:03:18.581 --> 00:03:21.107
So tan x is increasing on each branch,

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:22.712
not on its whole domain.

00:03:23.362 --> 00:03:25.237
Now a worked example from the course.

00:03:25.862 --> 00:03:28.068
Differentiate y equals tan of,

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:30.315
two x minus pi over three,

00:03:30.877 --> 00:03:33.104
and find its gradient at x equals zero.

00:03:33.928 --> 00:03:34.873
First, the picture.

00:03:35.516 --> 00:03:38.062
Two x minus pi over three is two

00:03:38.122 --> 00:03:40.508
lots of x minus pi over six.

00:03:41.151 --> 00:03:43.820
So this graph is tan x squashed sideways

00:03:43.881 --> 00:03:46.289
by a half, then slid right by pi

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:46.630
over six.

00:03:47.335 --> 00:03:48.563
You've seen this with exponentials.

00:03:49.224 --> 00:03:51.769
A sideways squash by a half doubles every

00:03:51.829 --> 00:03:52.951
slope at matching points,

00:03:53.452 --> 00:03:54.795
and a slide changes none.

00:03:55.499 --> 00:03:56.349
The walls move too.

00:03:56.896 --> 00:03:58.782
The squash pulls them in, and the slide

00:03:58.943 --> 00:04:01.271
moves them along, to minus pi over twelve

00:04:01.653 --> 00:04:02.816
and five pi over twelve.

00:04:03.829 --> 00:04:05.645
Now the algebra agrees with the picture.

00:04:06.129 --> 00:04:08.753
The outside is tan, and the inside is

00:04:08.835 --> 00:04:10.558
two x minus pi over three.

00:04:11.321 --> 00:04:13.992
The inside's derivative is two, because pi over

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:15.097
three is a constant.

00:04:15.922 --> 00:04:18.347
So the outside gives sec squared of the

00:04:18.388 --> 00:04:20.031
same inside, times two.

00:04:20.995 --> 00:04:23.358
d y by d x equals two sec

00:04:23.399 --> 00:04:26.024
squared of, two x minus pi over three.

00:04:26.787 --> 00:04:27.394
Where does it hold?

00:04:27.958 --> 00:04:30.704
Wherever the inside isn't pi over two plus

00:04:30.745 --> 00:04:31.386
k pi.

00:04:32.249 --> 00:04:32.688
Solving that,

00:04:33.251 --> 00:04:35.835
x can't be five pi over twelve, plus

00:04:35.876 --> 00:04:37.017
k pi over two.

00:04:38.101 --> 00:04:39.668
Now x equals zero.

00:04:40.171 --> 00:04:42.644
The inside is minus pi over three, and

00:04:42.725 --> 00:04:43.910
cos of that is a half.

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:46.060
So sec squared is four,

00:04:46.604 --> 00:04:48.591
and the gradient is two times four, which

00:04:48.653 --> 00:04:49.033
is eight.

00:04:49.736 --> 00:04:52.343
The tangent at zero, minus root three,

00:04:52.865 --> 00:04:53.165
agrees.

00:04:53.755 --> 00:04:55.767
Here's a quick check for any gradient of

00:04:55.868 --> 00:04:56.531
tan x.

00:04:57.257 --> 00:04:59.249
The slope of tan is one plus its

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:00.034
height squared.

00:05:00.598 --> 00:05:03.144
That's just sec squared written another way, because

00:05:03.305 --> 00:05:05.550
one plus tan squared equals sec squared.

00:05:06.273 --> 00:05:08.436
At pi over three, the height is root

00:05:08.478 --> 00:05:10.822
three, so the slope is one plus three,

00:05:11.244 --> 00:05:11.463
four.

00:05:12.307 --> 00:05:14.460
It works wherever tan is defined, with x

00:05:14.561 --> 00:05:15.144
in radians.

00:05:15.769 --> 00:05:18.450
With an inside, like two x minus pi

00:05:18.491 --> 00:05:18.950
over three,

00:05:19.452 --> 00:05:22.013
multiply by the inside's derivative as well.

00:05:24.692 --> 00:05:25.160
Your turn.

00:05:25.668 --> 00:05:28.112
Find the exact gradient of the curve y

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.658
equals tan x, at the point where x

00:05:30.839 --> 00:05:32.562
equals five pi over six.

00:05:33.350 --> 00:05:34.180
Pause here to answer.

00:05:45.015 --> 00:05:47.122
d y by d x is sec squared

00:05:47.204 --> 00:05:47.323
x.

00:05:47.966 --> 00:05:50.249
At five pi over six, cos x is

00:05:50.411 --> 00:05:53.215
minus root three over two, so cos squared

00:05:53.336 --> 00:05:55.739
x is three quarters, and sec squared x

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:56.721
is four thirds.

00:05:57.584 --> 00:05:59.908
Check with the height: tan is minus one

00:05:59.989 --> 00:06:02.253
over root three, and one plus a third

00:06:02.354 --> 00:06:03.135
is four thirds.

00:06:03.777 --> 00:06:05.482
The point is below the axis, but the

00:06:05.523 --> 00:06:06.806
curve is still rising.

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:09.690
Minus two over root three is sec x,

00:06:10.111 --> 00:06:12.172
not sec squared, and a tan slope is

00:06:12.333 --> 00:06:12.913
never negative.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:15.166
Last one.

00:06:15.756 --> 00:06:18.581
Differentiate y equals tan three x,

00:06:19.143 --> 00:06:20.926
and state the values of x for which

00:06:20.987 --> 00:06:22.108
the derivative exists.

00:06:22.816 --> 00:06:23.688
Pause here to answer.

00:06:34.408 --> 00:06:37.432
The inside is three x, with derivative three,

00:06:37.854 --> 00:06:40.077
so d y by d x is three

00:06:40.278 --> 00:06:41.580
sec squared three x.

00:06:42.222 --> 00:06:44.883
It exists wherever cos three x isn't zero,

00:06:45.164 --> 00:06:47.265
so three x can't be pi over two

00:06:47.386 --> 00:06:48.226
plus k pi.

00:06:49.007 --> 00:06:49.847
Dividing by three,

00:06:50.388 --> 00:06:52.669
x can't be pi over six, plus k

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:54.811
pi over three, for any integer k.

00:06:55.652 --> 00:06:58.177
Copying tan's own exclusion, x not equal to

00:06:58.218 --> 00:07:00.663
pi over two plus k pi, misses x

00:07:00.804 --> 00:07:01.845
equals pi over six.

00:07:03.508 --> 00:07:05.792
So tan x, a quotient of sine and

00:07:05.853 --> 00:07:08.919
cos, has derivative sec squared x, on each

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:09.360
branch.

00:07:10.224 --> 00:07:13.355
Next, the other three reciprocal functions: sec x,

00:07:13.698 --> 00:07:15.323
cosec x and cot x.

00:07:16.187 --> 00:07:18.755
Here's y equals sec x, which is one

00:07:18.916 --> 00:07:19.578
over cos x.

00:07:20.342 --> 00:07:22.290
Its walls are where cos x is zero,

00:07:22.753 --> 00:07:24.521
and at x equals zero it sits at

00:07:24.722 --> 00:07:24.822
one.

00:07:25.607 --> 00:07:27.497
How does its slope change, and where does

00:07:27.538 --> 00:07:28.483
its derivative exist?

00:07:31.368 --> 00:07:32.170
Here are the key notes.

00:07:32.814 --> 00:07:34.541
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:34.603 --> 00:07:35.084
to copy them.
